## Why This Is Asked
This interview question tests dynamic programming with prefix-state tracking and solution reconstruction. The inner minimum calculation forces careful leftward scanning, providing a tangible beginner-to-intermediate challenge while remaining implementable in O(n²k) time.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over prefixes with block count tracking
- On-the-fly maintenance of minimum values over sliding windows
- Solution reconstruction via backpointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k), Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
      let currentMin = Infinity;
      for (let p = i; p >= t; p--) {
        currentMin = Math.min(currentMin, A[p-1]);
        const candidate = (dp[p-1][t-1] + currentMin * (i-p+1));
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          pre[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = pre[i][t];
    blocks.unshift([p, i]);
    i = p - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a harder variant of partition DP where each block's value depends on its range (max-min). It requires careful state design, precomputation of subarray values, and backtracking to output exact k non-overlapping blocks.

## Key Concepts
- 2D DP over prefix index and number of blocks
- Precomputing block values V[l][r] = max - min for all subarrays
- Reconstruction via parent pointers to output endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRangeSubarrayValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const V = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[r]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      V[l][r] = mx - mn;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + V[j - 1][i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t] !== -1) {
    const j = parent[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constrained path DP with an extra state (turns). Encourages explicit DP state design, directional transitions, and plan for path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with 4D state (i, j, t, dir)
- Transition logic depends on last direction
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Edge cases when k is large vs. maximum turns (n+m-2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>
    Array.from({length:m}, ()=>
      Array.from({length:k+1}, ()=> Array(2).fill(NEG))))
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
        for(let dir=0;dir<2;dir++){
          const cur = dp[i][j][t][dir];
          if(cur===NEG) continue;
          // move right
          if(j+1<m){
            const nt = t + (dir===0?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i][j+1][nt][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][nt][0], cur + grid[i][j+1]);
          }
          // move down
          if(i+1<n){
            const nt = t + (dir===1?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i+1][j][nt][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][nt][1], cur + grid[i+1][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = NEG;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
    ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n-1][m-1][t][0], dp[n-1][m-1][t][1]);
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce space to O(n*m*k)?
- How does the solution change if k is unconstrained or if a minimum number of turns is required?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests advanced dynamic programming techniques involving multiple non-overlapping segments with per-element costs—a pattern frequently appearing in interviews for algorithm-intensive roles.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with fixed number of blocks requiring optimal segmentation
- Prefix sum transformations to simplify recurrence relations
- Time-space optimization strategies and solution reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValue(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i+1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const B = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let x = 0; x <= n; x++) B[x] = P[x] - c * x;

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i][t-1];
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + B[i] - B[j];
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && parent[curr][currT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[curr][currT];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    currT--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Implementation Details
- The transformation B[x] = P[x] - c*x captures the net value after maintenance costs
- The nested loop structure ensures we consider all possible segment boundaries
- Parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal segment endpoints

